From ecdee00f0ce99d03a80512ea53c20e049a30e8f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 06:26:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(android): draw the woven output whole-window, not per
 submit-time rect
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Device report after the browser#87 draw-back fix: no more 2D stamping, all
seven tiles weave, but during a scroll the woven content TRAILS its element by
about a frame.

That is the documented residual of the stage this arm was at, not a new bug.
The Windows lineage went 0022 -> 0046 for exactly this. 0022 introduced the
submit-time |drawn_rect| draw-back and says of it in as many words that it
"does not remove lag; it converts tearing/smear into coherent lateness". 0046
then DELETED that loop: once the runtime clears its window-sized output to
premultiplied transparent on the frame's first chunk (spec v5, in.firstChunk)
"gaps carry no stale/smear, so there is no longer a reason to scope the draw to
submit-time rects". The Android arm shipped 0022's half and never 0046's, so it
inherited 0022's residual the moment the draw-back started honouring
|drawn_rect|.

WHERE THE FRAME ENTERED - not where it was expected to. It is not a submit that
completes late, and the Android path is not structurally one frame behind:

  * xrWeaveSubmitDXR is synchronous end to end. SubmitBatchAndroid calls
    s.submit() inline and the service vkWaitForFences before replying
    (comp_multi_weave_android.c) - return IS completion.
  * that submit runs inside MaybeWeaveOutput(), after the page raster and
    before Flush/Submit, in the same GPU task as the draw.
  * Display::DrawAndSwap hands this frame's AGGREGATED rects to the GPU stage
    before renderer_->DrawFrame(), and the tracked channel is cc's draw-time
    projected visible_bounds, not the main-thread snapshot.

Submit, weave and draw-back are all one frame. The lateness came from the DRAW
consulting per-target state instead of the runtime's own output. |drawn_rect|
is promoted only for a target that took part in THIS frame's weave, so a target
momentarily absent from |inline_3d_rects_| - a re-raster, a lazy-activation
margin, a lost race, all of which a scroll produces constantly - kept LAST
frame's rect and was still drawn at it. One blipped frame per tile is one frame
of coherent lateness, which is what a human reads as a trail.

So stop asking a browser-side rect where the pixels are and let the runtime
answer: ONE whole-window kSrcOver of the woven output. It placed those pixels,
and its transparent clear means it contributes nothing where it wove nothing
this frame. kSrcOver is safe for the same reason as on Windows - patch 0050
pre-blends the page backdrop under the SBS, so a tile leaves the weave with
alpha = 1 and kSrcOver and kSrc coincide inside it.

The browser#87 stamping protection is not lost, it moves to where 0046 put it:
|wove_this_frame| gates the whole block, so a frame that wove nothing draws
nothing and the per-tile recovery draw paints those tiles mono. |pending_rect|
and |drawn_rect| stay maintained as the per-target record.

Cost on the GPU thread: one window-sized NEAREST sample replaces N per-tile
blits - fewer draw calls, more texels, well under a millisecond on this Adreno.
The frame is still dominated by the unchanged synchronous submit (~5.5-6.5 ms
at full res, one batch call for all rects).

AND COUNT WHAT WOVE, not what exists. Once the draw is one whole-window blit
there is no per-tile draw left to count, and reporting displayxr_targets_.size()
made the diagnostic read "drew back 6" on a frame where five tiles wove and one
did not — evidence pointing away from the very tile that was broken. |drawn_rect|
is non-empty exactly for the targets that took part in THIS frame's weave, so
count those.

